UPLOAD CEILING — runtime re-proof after the fix
================================================
route   POST /api/workspaces/:id/documents/upload   (origin http://127.0.0.1:3002,
        Origin header https://172.20.10.2:3001, organizationId=no-such-org)
reading a 401 UNAUTHORIZED means the multipart body PARSED and the request reached
authorization — which is the only thing under test. A 400 "Malformed multipart
body." means the body arrived truncated.

BEFORE (Phase 6 HEAD, experimental.proxyClientMaxBodySize unset — bisect, /tmp/ceiling.mjs)
  largest multipart body that PARSES: 10485760 bytes (10.000 MiB)   -> 401
  smallest that is refused:           10489856 bytes (10.004 MiB)   -> 400 Malformed multipart body.
  json 8 MB  -> parsed
  json 12 MB -> refused the same way
  The boundary is DEFAULT_BODY_CLONE_SIZE_LIMIT in next/dist/server/body-streams.js,
  reached because proxy.ts's matcher covers /api/*, so Next clones every API body.
  Advertised ceilings: 100 MiB (DOCUMENT_INGESTION_LIMITS.maxUploadBytes) and
  110 MiB (app/api/jobs/route.ts). Both were unreachable by a factor of ten, and
  the refusal blamed the user's file.

AFTER (audit HEAD a14e7c1, experimental.proxyClientMaxBodySize = "120mb")
   22 MiB -> 401 {"error":{"code":"UNAUTHORIZED","message":"Authentication is required.","requestId":"caaf7562-ddd5-461d-b160-a2961e77e54   (35ms)
   60 MiB -> 401 {"error":{"code":"UNAUTHORIZED","message":"Authentication is required.","requestId":"b3fa3d00-b0a3-465f-8ca0-ff09c600fa7   (50ms)
  101 MiB -> 413 {"error":{"code":"PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE","message":"Upload too large.","requestId":"75d75e68-e601-41e9-bb92-f084a0da120b"}}   (34ms)

  22 MiB and 60 MiB now reach authorization: the 10 MiB truncation is gone.
  101 MiB is refused by the product's own content-length gate with the honest
  PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE / "Upload too large." — not by silent truncation, and not with
  a message about a malformed file.

Pinned by test: proxy.test.ts, "the body-clone limit clears every advertised upload
ceiling" (3 cases) — reads next.config.mjs, DOCUMENT_INGESTION_LIMITS and the jobs
route's own literal, so raising a ceiling without raising the clone limit fails.
